\title{mediaPlanSim: Synthetic Multi-Insertion Exposure Data with Known Truth}
\description{
Survey data on media consumption rarely record exposure to several
insertions of the same vehicle, so exposure-distribution models cannot be
validated against an observed distribution of contacts. This package
generates synthetic respondents from explicit data-generating mechanisms
(see \code{\link[=scenario_independent]{scenario_independent()}}, \code{\link[=scenario_gaussian]{scenario_gaussian()}} and
\code{\link[=scenario_latent_class]{scenario_latent_class()}}), simulates their contacts with
\code{\link[=simulate_exposure]{simulate_exposure()}}, and returns the true distribution of contacts and the
population moments of the mechanism with \code{\link[=exposure_truth]{exposure_truth()}} and
\code{\link[=population_moments]{population_moments()}}. The inputs of a model (reach of one and two
insertions, duplication) can be taken from the truth or estimated from the
simulated sample with \code{\link[=sample_moments]{sample_moments()}}, and the model output can be scored
with \code{\link[=distribution_error]{distribution_error()}}.
}
\details{
\strong{What the package is not.} Data simulated from a mechanism inherit that
mechanism's assumptions. A model that reproduces the truth of a mechanism has
not thereby been shown to describe real audiences. The package is a tool to
study how models behave under controlled and documented conditions.
}
